    1	"""
    2	Proposal B5: Self-correcting recursive halving p(n) -> p(n/2).
    3	
    4	Define theta(n) := p(n) - 2*p(floor(n/2)).  By PNT,
    5	    p(n)   ~ n log n,
    6	    2*p(n/2) ~ 2 (n/2) log(n/2) = n log n - n log 2,
    7	so theta(n) ~ n log 2 + (lower-order chaotic).
    8	
    9	Question: is the chaotic part of theta(n) *smaller* than the chaotic
    10	part of p(n) alone?  If yes, recursive halving can reduce the per-step
    11	unpredictability and yield a polylog algorithm via depth-log-n recursion.
    12	
    13	This script:
    14	  1. computes theta(n) for n = 100, 200, ..., n_max.
    15	  2. fits theta(n) = c_0 + c_1 n + c_2 n log n + c_3 n log log n
    16	                   + c_4 sqrt(n) + (residual).
    17	  3. measures residual scaling as a function of n (does residual / n^alpha
    18	     vanish for some alpha < 1?).
    19	
    20	Decision rule:
    21	  - residual ~ const  ->  theta is *smoothly* predictable (WIN)
    22	  - residual ~ n^{0.5}  ->  reduces to original problem (CLOSED)
    23	  - residual ~ n^a, 0 < a < 0.5  ->  ambiguous (refine the experiment)
    24	
    25	Usage:
    26	    python3 halving_recursion.py
    27	    python3 halving_recursion.py --n_max 50000 --step 100
    28	"""
